    "finalPromptText": "I'm drafting the section of my review that discusses Kuang's use of etymological footnotes and how she weaves real linguistic scholarship into the narrative. Can you find any interviews or articles where she talks about her research process for the silver-working translation magic system?",
    "finalAssistantVisibleText": "In an interview with Literary Hub, R.F. Kuang discusses her extensive research process for *Babel*. She mentions spending months immersed in 19th-century linguistic scholarship, the colonial history of the British Empire, and accounts of Oxford's imperial entanglements. \n\nKuang explains that the silver-working magic system in the novel is designed to literalize the idea that translation inherently involves loss and extraction. She states, \"Every act of rendering meaning across languages destroys something in the original.\" This highlights how the empire relies on individuals who are bilingual or multilingual to navigate the gaps between languages, which she argues is where the magic works.\n\nAdditionally, she draws attention to the history of Oriental studies at Oxford and how translation has been used in 19th-century missionary and commercial activities. Kuang criticizes how footnotes in philological texts of that time often erased indigenous knowledge by crediting only European intermediaries. The subtitle of the book, *An Arcane History of the Oxford Translators' Revolution*, reflects her goal of writing a counter-history that reveals what traditional institutional histories tend to obscure.\n\nThis exploration not only underscores the thematic elements of language and power in *Babel* but also enriches the narrative with depth derived from real historical contexts. For further insights, you can read the full interview [here](https://openbooks.net/interviews/rf-kuang-babel-research).",
    "finalAssistantRawText": "In an interview with Literary Hub, R.F. Kuang discusses her extensive research process for *Babel*. She mentions spending months immersed in 19th-century linguistic scholarship, the colonial history of the British Empire, and accounts of Oxford's imperial entanglements. \n\nKuang explains that the silver-working magic system in the novel is designed to literalize the idea that translation inherently involves loss and extraction. She states, \"Every act of rendering meaning across languages destroys something in the original.\" This highlights how the empire relies on individuals who are bilingual or multilingual to navigate the gaps between languages, which she argues is where the magic works.\n\nAdditionally, she draws attention to the history of Oriental studies at Oxford and how translation has been used in 19th-century missionary and commercial activities. Kuang criticizes how footnotes in philological texts of that time often erased indigenous knowledge by crediting only European intermediaries. The subtitle of the book, *An Arcane History of the Oxford Translators' Revolution*, reflects her goal of writing a counter-history that reveals what traditional institutional histories tend to obscure.\n\nThis exploration not only underscores the thematic elements of language and power in *Babel* but also enriches the narrative with depth derived from real historical contexts. For further insights, you can read the full interview [here](https://openbooks.net/interviews/rf-kuang-babel-research).",
