    {
      "text": "Here is how the first chapter of *Babel* opens:\n\n---\n\n**Book I — Chapter 1**\n\nBy the time the plague came to Canton, the boy's mother was already weak. She had sent two letters to the factories where his father had once worked, but no one remembered his name. In the third week of the fever she grew so light that he could lift her with one arm. On the fourth she stopped speaking, and on the fifth she stopped breathing, which astonished him less than he would have expected. For a long time he sat beside the mat with her hand in his and listened to the cicadas in the courtyard wall.\n\nA man arrived in the afternoon — an Englishman, tall and thin and gleaming with sweat, attended by two Cantonese clerks. He wore a coat of dark grey wool, unsuited to the city's damp heat, and a pair of spectacles whose lenses caught the light. He stood in the doorway a long time before he spoke, considering the boy with the detached interest of a man examining a silver bar at the assay office.\n\n'You speak English,' the Englishman said. It was not a question. It was a fact that had been reported to him, presumably by one of the neighbours in the lane, and he was confirming it.\n\nThe boy did speak English. His mother had taught him as a child, using a worn grammar…\n\n---\n\nThis passage establishes a tone of sorrow and detachment, showcasing the fragility of life and the impact of colonial presence. Kuang's narrative voice reflects both emotional depth and an intriguing setting from the very beginning, effectively setting up the themes of loss and the complexities of language and cultural exchange that will unfold throughout the book. You can analyze the pacing and tone as you discuss Robin Swift's introduction and the context around his character.",
